1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:05,000
Hello and welcome to this lecture on BioRisk Assessment.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,600
In this lecture, I will be discussing

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:12,600
the various guidelines and the pathogen safety data sheets

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,040
and their application to risk assessment

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,720
of biological agents.

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,840
All of these lectures can be downloaded, reused,

7
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:26,540
repurposed and remixed and shared as they are distributed

8
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:34,710
under a Creative Commons Attribution License.

9
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:39,730
My earlier lectures focused on the AMP cycle,

10
00:00:39,850 --> 00:00:42,130
which is the risk assessment, risk mitigation

11
00:00:42,130 --> 00:00:45,940
and performance assessment cycle.

12
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:48,260
During the process of risk assessment,

13
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:52,960
we commence with hazard identification.

14
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,760
However, we also need specific guidelines.

15
00:00:56,760 --> 00:01:01,000
With this in mind, I have delved into the WHO laboratory

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,320
biosafety manual in an earlier lecture.

17
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:05,380
And in this lecture, we will be delving

18
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:08,280
into the pathogen safety data sheets,

19
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:16,250
which is a very useful document for BioRisk managers.

20
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:17,930
These are your learning objectives

21
00:01:17,930 --> 00:01:19,330
for this current lecture.

22
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:22,590
I will introduce you to the process of risk assessment

23
00:01:22,590 --> 00:01:24,730
for a known biological agent.

24
00:01:24,730 --> 00:01:27,770
I will discuss the key points that must be addressed

25
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:29,330
during the risk assessment.

26
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:32,490
And I will discuss the pathogen safety data sheets,

27
00:01:32,490 --> 00:01:38,840
which are the PSDS from the government of Canada.

28
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:40,880
These are your learning outcomes.

29
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:42,340
Upon completion of this module,

30
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:43,780
you should demonstrate the ability

31
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:47,380
to develop an instrument for risk assessment.

32
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:50,980
Now this instrument is in the form of a survey

33
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:54,620
in order to conduct a biological risk assessment.

34
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:57,780
And we reference the WHO-LBM.

35
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:00,580
So I'll refer to the World Health Organization

36
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:02,340
laboratory biosafety manual

37
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:07,100
using this particular acronym WHO-LBM throughout this module.

38
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:10,920
We will address specific criteria

39
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:13,880
and you will learn how to address these criteria

40
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:18,460
as risk assessment is based on the biological agent

41
00:02:18,460 --> 00:02:23,680
that you are currently intending to work with.

42
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,120
And finally, we will apply,

43
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:27,400
you will learn how to apply the information

44
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:30,320
from the PSDS to assess the risks

45
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,320
posed by a known biological agent.

46
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:34,600
So these should be your learning outcomes,

47
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:37,200
which you must focus upon

48
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:41,890
during this particular module.

49
00:02:41,890 --> 00:02:43,450
When we discuss risk assessment,

50
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:46,330
we always look at what is known as the scope.

51
00:02:46,330 --> 00:02:48,450
Now there are risks that are within the scope

52
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:50,010
of this assessment.

53
00:02:50,010 --> 00:02:53,370
So you have your iceberg and this is what you see.

54
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:55,750
And this is what you can assess.

55
00:02:55,750 --> 00:02:58,150
And then you can address this

56
00:02:58,150 --> 00:03:00,090
by applying the pertinent controls.

57
00:03:00,090 --> 00:03:02,210
However, there are risks that are beyond

58
00:03:02,210 --> 00:03:03,790
the scope of the assessment.

59
00:03:03,790 --> 00:03:05,550
And this is what you do not see.

60
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:08,270
For instance, if you are working

61
00:03:08,270 --> 00:03:12,410
with a risk group to biological agent,

62
00:03:12,410 --> 00:03:16,650
and one of your laboratory workers has an allergy,

63
00:03:16,650 --> 00:03:19,490
which he or she has not reported earlier

64
00:03:19,490 --> 00:03:21,090
because they were not aware of it,

65
00:03:21,090 --> 00:03:24,530
exposure to this RG2 biological agent

66
00:03:24,530 --> 00:03:28,450
can cause morbidity or even mortality.

67
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:31,610
So these are risks which you must anticipate,

68
00:03:31,610 --> 00:03:33,930
but they are what are known as unknown risks

69
00:03:33,930 --> 00:03:42,150
that are beyond the scope of assessment.

70
00:03:42,150 --> 00:03:45,150
We will now look at microbiological risk assessment

71
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:48,310
based on the WHO laboratory biosafety manual

72
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:49,950
and discuss the development

73
00:03:49,950 --> 00:03:54,710
of an instrument for risk assessment.

74
00:03:54,710 --> 00:03:57,750
These are some of the factors which you must consider

75
00:03:57,750 --> 00:04:00,430
when you conduct a risk assessment.

76
00:04:00,430 --> 00:04:02,190
The first one is the pathogenicity

77
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:05,350
of the biological agent and the infectious dose.

78
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:09,510
Is the microorganism a pathogen

79
00:04:09,510 --> 00:04:13,610
which has been reported to cause mortality and mobility?

80
00:04:13,610 --> 00:04:15,610
And what is the infectious dose?

81
00:04:15,610 --> 00:04:18,490
How many colony forming units

82
00:04:18,490 --> 00:04:23,490
or particles of this particular microorganism

83
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:28,170
are sufficient in order to cause infection?

84
00:04:28,170 --> 00:04:31,330
And this varies from individual to individual,

85
00:04:31,330 --> 00:04:35,190
from environmental condition to environmental condition,

86
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:36,990
but you must assess this risk

87
00:04:36,990 --> 00:04:40,650
in terms of the pathogenicity and infectious dose.

88
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:44,090
The second aspect is the potential outcome of exposure.

89
00:04:44,090 --> 00:04:48,070
Does it result in morbidity, mortality?

90
00:04:48,070 --> 00:04:51,350
What is the natural route of infection?

91
00:04:51,350 --> 00:04:53,990
Is it via the nasal passages?

92
00:04:53,990 --> 00:04:56,290
Is it through the skin?

93
00:04:56,290 --> 00:04:58,430
Or is it subdermal below the skin?

94
00:04:58,430 --> 00:04:59,950
Or is it via ingestion?

95
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:02,030
What is the route of infection?

96
00:05:02,030 --> 00:05:03,870
What are the other routes of infection?

97
00:05:03,870 --> 00:05:06,010
Does it have multiple routes of infection?

98
00:05:06,990 --> 00:05:10,770
And are these the result of laboratory manipulation?

99
00:05:10,770 --> 00:05:15,770
So for instance, if you are culturing a lab pathogen,

100
00:05:17,830 --> 00:05:20,670
basically cultivating a pathogen

101
00:05:20,670 --> 00:05:22,790
in the laboratory using enriched media,

102
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:26,810
if there is a spill in the lab with the enriched media,

103
00:05:26,810 --> 00:05:31,810
this particular pathogen is more likely to cause harm

104
00:05:31,830 --> 00:05:33,030
to the laboratory workers

105
00:05:33,030 --> 00:05:37,030
because you have increased the culture volume

106
00:05:37,030 --> 00:05:40,110
and this represents laboratory manipulation.

107
00:05:40,110 --> 00:05:42,390
Then we look at the stability of the biological agent

108
00:05:42,390 --> 00:05:43,230
in the environment.

109
00:05:43,230 --> 00:05:45,110
For instance, in the case of coronavirus,

110
00:05:45,110 --> 00:05:48,550
there have been multiple reports of stability studies

111
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:52,590
reporting that the virus may be stable for longer

112
00:05:52,590 --> 00:05:56,990
than other viruses for a longer duration of time

113
00:05:56,990 --> 00:05:58,290
in the environment.

114
00:05:58,290 --> 00:06:01,070
Then we look at the concentration and culture volume

115
00:06:01,070 --> 00:06:02,610
of the biological agent.

116
00:06:02,610 --> 00:06:04,910
Now, as a general principle,

117
00:06:04,910 --> 00:06:07,610
when working with pathogens in the laboratory,

118
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:12,250
we must minimize this culture volume

119
00:06:12,250 --> 00:06:14,770
because a very high culture volume can lead

120
00:06:14,770 --> 00:06:19,770
to potential spills and the consequent infections.

121
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:27,280
We also look at the presence of a suitable host.

122
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:31,000
For instance, if you are cultivating a parasite

123
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,020
in the laboratory,

124
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:35,040
if there is no mosquito over the vector

125
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:39,840
to transmit this parasite from the animal into the human,

126
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:43,440
the likelihood of infection and transmission

127
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:45,320
is very, very low.

128
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:47,620
We also look at a priori information,

129
00:06:47,620 --> 00:06:49,440
which is available from research papers

130
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:54,080
and clinical reports and epidemiological studies.

131
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:56,840
We look at the laboratory procedures during risk assessment.

132
00:06:56,840 --> 00:07:00,000
Does it involve centrifugation, aerosolization

133
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,560
of the content using pipettes

134
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:05,360
or does it involve any other procedures

135
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:06,940
such as microscopy,

136
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:10,560
which can pose a risk to the laboratory user.

137
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:13,640
Any genetic manipulation has the microbe

138
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:16,880
or the biological agent being genetically modified.

139
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:21,200
And we look at local availability of effective profile axis,

140
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:22,860
which is preventive treatment,

141
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:29,800
or therapeutic interventions for this particular pathogen.

142
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:32,040
This is a risk instrument.

143
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:33,360
It's an example,

144
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:36,240
and you may have to adapt this for your own laboratory

145
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
for your virus management scenario.

146
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:42,120
The first question is,

147
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:46,960
has the biological agent been reported to be a pathogen?

148
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:50,280
So if newly emerging pathogens

149
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:54,120
may not have been reported earlier,

150
00:07:54,120 --> 00:07:56,800
and this may lead to lack of alertness

151
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:59,560
on the part of the virus managers.

152
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:03,940
So every biological agent must be treated with caution

153
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:05,600
based on earlier reports

154
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:08,240
as well as currently developing situations.

155
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:13,640
Does exposure to the biological agent lead to morbidity?

156
00:08:13,640 --> 00:08:17,360
So you have, so if this is yes, and this is yes,

157
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:20,160
so morbidity means your laboratory workers

158
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,480
are likely to get sick if they're exposed

159
00:08:24,760 --> 00:08:26,440
and may have to be hospitalized.

160
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:30,280
Is the root of infection via inhalation?

161
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:32,720
If yes, then you click yes.

162
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:36,320
Is the root of infection via contact?

163
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:38,000
No, then you click no.

164
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:41,840
Is the biological agent stable in the environment?

165
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:43,240
Of course, if you pose this question,

166
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:45,760
you have to pose other questions such as if yes,

167
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:48,680
then how long, how many seconds?

168
00:08:48,680 --> 00:08:51,680
And is it viable on surfaces?

169
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:57,400
Or is it viable in the air at that local temperature?

170
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:00,120
Has the biological agent been genetically modified?

171
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:03,960
This can be obtained by our genome sequencing reports.

172
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:06,880
And is the treatment readily available?

173
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:09,580
These are some of the key questions

174
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:12,120
which you must address when you develop

175
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:14,700
instrument for risk assessment.

176
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:19,160
We now move on to the next,

177
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:21,920
and this must be discussed with your

178
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:26,440
principal investigators and researchers at your laboratory.

179
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:30,320
So the first one is basically related to training.

180
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:33,720
Has adequate training been provided?

181
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:36,880
The terminology used here is adequate.

182
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:38,080
Adequate or sufficient,

183
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:42,760
you can refer to the specific definition of adequate

184
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:47,760
in the CWA 15793 Laboratory By-Risk Management Manual.

185
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:52,960
Adequate contingency plans.

186
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:55,200
This related to accidental spills.

187
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:58,480
So if you have said yes and yes,

188
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:00,580
the risk is significantly lower.

189
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:04,760
Facility equipped with essential equipment.

190
00:10:04,760 --> 00:10:06,080
So as I mentioned earlier,

191
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:09,480
the essential equipment is a biological safety cabinet

192
00:10:10,760 --> 00:10:14,120
based on the risk groups, the four risk groups.

193
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:16,640
So you require at least a basic

194
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:18,720
laboratory biological safety cabinet.

195
00:10:18,720 --> 00:10:20,360
And if you have yes, you click here.

196
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:24,060
And then if no, you proceed to the next question.

197
00:10:24,100 --> 00:10:25,700
Is PPE available?

198
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:28,100
And the type of PPE.

199
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:30,220
So in our lecture on PPEs

200
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:31,980
and personal protective equipment,

201
00:10:31,980 --> 00:10:35,340
I will discuss the various kind of PPEs available

202
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:38,580
to mitigate the risks posed by inhalation,

203
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:42,480
via cutaneous or subcutaneous entry,

204
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:46,140
and other routes of entry.

205
00:10:46,140 --> 00:10:48,820
The next one is related to secondary containment

206
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:51,620
is the facility equipped with a waste disposal system

207
00:10:51,620 --> 00:10:53,620
or effluent disposal system.

208
00:10:53,620 --> 00:10:59,750
I will discuss this in the engineering controls.

209
00:10:59,750 --> 00:11:02,190
Number six is, is your facility secure

210
00:11:02,190 --> 00:11:03,910
in terms of biosecurity?

211
00:11:03,910 --> 00:11:07,030
And finally, do we have specific SOPs

212
00:11:07,030 --> 00:11:08,990
to manage the pathogen?

213
00:11:08,990 --> 00:11:11,150
For instance, if you're working in a laboratory

214
00:11:11,150 --> 00:11:13,430
with mycobacterium tuberculosis,

215
00:11:13,430 --> 00:11:15,830
and in the current situation,

216
00:11:15,830 --> 00:11:18,030
you have an emergency and you have to suddenly change

217
00:11:18,030 --> 00:11:21,230
to working with coronavirus.

218
00:11:21,230 --> 00:11:23,830
The SOPs which are applicable

219
00:11:23,830 --> 00:11:25,990
to mycobacterium tuberculosis

220
00:11:26,590 --> 00:11:28,310
will not be applicable to coronavirus.

221
00:11:28,310 --> 00:11:31,390
So you will have to revise and retrain your staff

222
00:11:31,390 --> 00:11:34,830
and develop new SOPs to address the new

223
00:11:34,830 --> 00:11:40,510
and emerging biological risk.

224
00:11:40,510 --> 00:11:43,190
We now move on to the pathogen safety data sheets

225
00:11:43,190 --> 00:11:45,150
and we must appreciate the efforts

226
00:11:45,150 --> 00:11:46,350
of the government of Canada

227
00:11:46,350 --> 00:11:48,550
in developing an excellent manual

228
00:11:48,550 --> 00:11:51,630
for laboratory users and virus managers.

229
00:11:51,630 --> 00:11:54,030
Now this pathogen safety data sheet,

230
00:11:54,030 --> 00:11:55,710
if you are a chemist, you will recall

231
00:11:55,710 --> 00:12:00,110
there is something known as a material safety data sheet

232
00:12:00,110 --> 00:12:04,350
and that discusses the various chemicals

233
00:12:04,350 --> 00:12:07,390
and their combustibilities and safety elements.

234
00:12:07,390 --> 00:12:09,150
And this pathogen safety data sheet

235
00:12:09,150 --> 00:12:11,790
focuses specifically on biological agents.

236
00:12:11,790 --> 00:12:14,590
Let us look at the various sections

237
00:12:14,590 --> 00:12:19,120
and I will discuss them in detail.

238
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:21,320
So this is the pathogen safety data sheet.

239
00:12:21,320 --> 00:12:22,960
You can access it at the website.

240
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:24,560
It is not in the form of a document.

241
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:26,160
It's in the form of a living document

242
00:12:26,160 --> 00:12:29,600
which is updated constantly by the government of Canada.

243
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:32,240
So thank you very much to the government of Canada

244
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:34,360
for developing this excellent resource material

245
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:39,360
for public use.

246
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:42,200
And this is the pathogen safety data sheet app.

247
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:44,200
It comes from Health Canada,

248
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:45,920
also from the government of Canada.

249
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:50,100
Another excellent resource for your bench.

250
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:53,200
You can use it on your benchtop with your mobile phone.

251
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:55,080
However, please do not take your mobile phone

252
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:57,360
into a biosafety level three laboratory

253
00:12:57,360 --> 00:13:02,360
as it can lead to transmission of infections via contact.

254
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:05,360
So this is a pathogen safety data sheet app.

255
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:12,600
You can find it on the Android and Apple stores.

256
00:13:12,600 --> 00:13:16,480
Let us delve into the various sections of the PSDS.

257
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:17,920
And this is the template

258
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:22,080
which is utilized for the development of the PSDS.

259
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:25,080
So this template can be used to develop PSDS

260
00:13:25,080 --> 00:13:28,280
for emerging pathogens as well as known pathogens.

261
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:33,280
The first section focuses on the infectious agent itself.

262
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:35,600
So we have the name, the common name,

263
00:13:35,600 --> 00:13:38,280
for instance, micro bacterium tuberculosis.

264
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:40,920
And you will have agent type, which is bacterium.

265
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:43,760
Taxonomy will list the taxonomy,

266
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:46,120
the group it belongs to, the genus species

267
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:47,280
for scientific usage.

268
00:13:47,280 --> 00:13:48,920
And these are the synonyms,

269
00:13:48,920 --> 00:13:50,500
other commonly used names

270
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:52,600
for that particular biological agent.

271
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:55,080
The characteristics of the biological agent,

272
00:13:55,080 --> 00:13:58,680
such as its infection cycle.

273
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:01,120
It's basically a life cycle

274
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:03,400
as well as the route of transmission,

275
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:06,200
a brief description of the biological agent

276
00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:10,160
as well as the properties related to the biological agent.

277
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:13,600
So the section one basically focuses on the type

278
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:17,760
of culture which is being used in most laboratories.

279
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:20,240
For instance, in microbial laboratories,

280
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:23,920
the general practice is to culture the microbial agent

281
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:27,640
on specific enriched medium or selective medium.

282
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:29,880
And this manual provides insights

283
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:37,700
into the infectious agent and its culture characteristics.

284
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:40,760
Section two focuses on hazard identification.

285
00:14:40,760 --> 00:14:45,760
In this section, we focus on pathogenicity and toxicity.

286
00:14:45,940 --> 00:14:47,980
The word toxicity has been used here

287
00:14:47,980 --> 00:14:51,420
because it also concerns biological toxins,

288
00:14:51,420 --> 00:14:55,100
communicability, transmission in a community-based setting,

289
00:14:55,160 --> 00:15:00,180
epidemiology, the host range.

290
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:02,740
Is it a zoonotic virus or biological agent

291
00:15:02,740 --> 00:15:05,960
or is it confined to homo sapiens or humans?

292
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:08,460
The infectious dose.

293
00:15:08,460 --> 00:15:12,460
Now this term infectious dose is used routinely.

294
00:15:12,460 --> 00:15:14,300
However, the actual definition

295
00:15:14,300 --> 00:15:18,300
for infectious dose is not very clear.

296
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:20,420
For instance, we cannot conduct what is known

297
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:23,480
as a Cox postulate study with human subjects

298
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:25,580
as it is a breach of ethics.

299
00:15:25,580 --> 00:15:27,340
So infectious doses, for instance,

300
00:15:27,340 --> 00:15:29,820
you cultivate a virus in the lab,

301
00:15:29,820 --> 00:15:32,820
and you have, for instance, 10,000 viral particles

302
00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:36,340
per cc of the medium.

303
00:15:36,340 --> 00:15:39,140
And then you expose a laboratory animal

304
00:15:39,140 --> 00:15:41,420
using a Muran study, for instance, a mouse.

305
00:15:41,420 --> 00:15:43,700
You expose it to the biological agent

306
00:15:43,700 --> 00:15:45,340
and then you check for infection.

307
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:47,260
So you do a graded study.

308
00:15:47,260 --> 00:15:50,380
You see 10,000, 20,000, 30,000, 40,000

309
00:15:50,380 --> 00:15:54,580
and check the dosage which causes an infection.

310
00:15:54,580 --> 00:15:57,660
Now this is actually termed as the infectious dose.

311
00:15:57,660 --> 00:16:00,860
However, these studies are not really indicative

312
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:04,360
as there'll be a variation between human subjects

313
00:16:04,360 --> 00:16:06,560
as well as mice.

314
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:09,060
And this should be treated with caution.

315
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:11,780
This is the infectious dose.

316
00:16:11,780 --> 00:16:14,220
And then you have incubation.

317
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:18,140
Incubation is the duration between actual exposure

318
00:16:18,140 --> 00:16:20,660
to the biological agent and the manifestation

319
00:16:20,660 --> 00:16:24,140
of the symptoms in terms of morbidity or mortality.

320
00:16:26,940 --> 00:16:29,380
Section three focuses on dissemination

321
00:16:29,380 --> 00:16:31,740
or distribution of the pathogen.

322
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:34,180
For instance, if you have a dengue virus,

323
00:16:34,180 --> 00:16:36,780
is there a reservoir for the virus?

324
00:16:36,780 --> 00:16:39,300
Is there a case of zoonosis or reverse zoonosis?

325
00:16:39,300 --> 00:16:43,420
This means is the pathogen transmitted

326
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:46,460
from animals to humans and vice versa

327
00:16:46,460 --> 00:16:48,900
from human to animal or not?

328
00:16:48,900 --> 00:16:50,740
These are questions which must be addressed

329
00:16:50,740 --> 00:16:54,020
in risk assessment and they have been delineated

330
00:16:54,020 --> 00:16:55,520
in the PSDS.

331
00:16:55,560 --> 00:16:58,360
And then the presence of suitable vectors.

332
00:16:58,360 --> 00:17:01,080
Certain viruses may complete their life cycle

333
00:17:01,080 --> 00:17:05,640
in specific vectors such as sleeping sickness, malaria,

334
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:07,520
and the dengue.

335
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:10,520
And this may require the presence of the vector in order

336
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:17,600
for them to be disseminated across the population.

337
00:17:17,600 --> 00:17:21,460
Section four focuses on stability and viability.

338
00:17:21,460 --> 00:17:23,780
And this relates to environmental stability

339
00:17:23,780 --> 00:17:26,060
as well as stability in the host,

340
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:28,220
drugs susceptibility.

341
00:17:28,220 --> 00:17:30,580
As you must be well aware by now,

342
00:17:30,580 --> 00:17:35,580
a large number of microbes are basically resistant to drugs

343
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:37,540
and they have developed what is known

344
00:17:37,540 --> 00:17:40,180
as antibiotic resistance profiles.

345
00:17:40,180 --> 00:17:44,220
And we must ascertain this in order to prevent

346
00:17:44,220 --> 00:17:45,820
laboratory acquired infections

347
00:17:45,820 --> 00:17:48,220
and hospital acquired infections.

348
00:17:48,220 --> 00:17:50,140
We also look at drug resistance,

349
00:17:50,980 --> 00:17:53,020
susceptibility to disinfectants.

350
00:17:53,020 --> 00:17:56,020
I will be discussing this in a lecture on disinfection.

351
00:17:56,020 --> 00:17:59,060
So when we expose a biological agent

352
00:17:59,060 --> 00:18:00,680
to a specific disinfectant,

353
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:02,780
there is something known as contact time

354
00:18:02,780 --> 00:18:06,040
or the duration for which the biological agent

355
00:18:06,040 --> 00:18:09,380
must be exposed to that particular sterilizing

356
00:18:09,380 --> 00:18:13,340
or disinfecting agent as well as the concentration.

357
00:18:13,340 --> 00:18:14,780
So this must be test.

358
00:18:14,780 --> 00:18:16,540
There are studies which have been conducted

359
00:18:16,540 --> 00:18:20,500
for various biological agents, scientific reports related

360
00:18:20,500 --> 00:18:23,180
to susceptibility to disinfectant.

361
00:18:23,180 --> 00:18:25,500
We look at physical inactivation

362
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:27,860
at what temperature is in inactivated.

363
00:18:27,860 --> 00:18:30,260
For instance, in the case of coronavirus,

364
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:31,940
when we receive a patient sample,

365
00:18:31,940 --> 00:18:35,900
we heat the sample to around 60 degrees Celsius

366
00:18:35,900 --> 00:18:38,900
in a heat block prior to processing.

367
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:40,940
And this inactivates the virus.

368
00:18:40,940 --> 00:18:44,460
So each biological agent will have its own characteristics

369
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:46,180
for physical inactivation.

370
00:18:46,180 --> 00:18:48,940
And this can be done in the form of chemical inactivation

371
00:18:48,940 --> 00:18:52,700
as well as heat treatment or UV radiation

372
00:18:52,700 --> 00:18:54,000
as the case may be.

373
00:18:54,040 --> 00:18:55,280
And there is another factor

374
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:57,780
which is survival outside of the host.

375
00:18:57,780 --> 00:19:02,360
For instance, bacillus anthracis forms spores.

376
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:05,360
So the bacillus itself will not survive well

377
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:06,360
in the environment.

378
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:09,640
It needs an enriched medium or at least a soil

379
00:19:09,640 --> 00:19:12,440
which is containing sufficient nutrient in order to grow.

380
00:19:12,440 --> 00:19:15,000
However, when it sporulates, it forms spores.

381
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:18,040
These spores remain viable in the environment

382
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:20,880
and they can be dispersed via air streams

383
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:24,320
and they can enter into human hosts

384
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:26,000
and infect human hosts, okay?

385
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,440
So these are factors which must be considered.

386
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:31,200
Survival outside the host must focus

387
00:19:31,200 --> 00:19:36,650
on these correlation characteristics as well.

388
00:19:36,650 --> 00:19:42,170
Section five focuses on first aid and medical treatment.

389
00:19:42,170 --> 00:19:45,610
So we focus on surveillance, medical surveillance.

390
00:19:45,610 --> 00:19:47,870
This may involve a population study.

391
00:19:49,330 --> 00:19:50,870
In your respective countries,

392
00:19:50,870 --> 00:19:53,010
you may have a surveillance system

393
00:19:53,010 --> 00:19:55,250
and certain pathogens are classified

394
00:19:55,250 --> 00:19:57,170
as notifiable pathogens.

395
00:19:57,170 --> 00:20:00,410
So for instance, if a clinic reports a case of cholera

396
00:20:00,410 --> 00:20:01,890
in a specific country

397
00:20:01,890 --> 00:20:06,890
and it's basically notifiable disease,

398
00:20:07,090 --> 00:20:09,450
the medical health practitioner

399
00:20:09,450 --> 00:20:11,850
must notify the local health authority

400
00:20:11,850 --> 00:20:13,770
and this will in turn be transmitted

401
00:20:13,770 --> 00:20:19,430
across the network chain.

402
00:20:19,430 --> 00:20:22,490
We also focus on first aid and treatment,

403
00:20:22,490 --> 00:20:26,370
the presence or the availability of an effective vaccine

404
00:20:26,370 --> 00:20:31,090
and profile access as well, preventive treatment.

405
00:20:31,090 --> 00:20:32,890
Generally in a laboratory setting,

406
00:20:32,890 --> 00:20:37,890
laboratory workers are inoculated with a vaccine

407
00:20:39,570 --> 00:20:41,690
against specific biological agents

408
00:20:41,690 --> 00:20:44,050
which your laboratory may be working with

409
00:20:44,050 --> 00:20:46,610
in order to prevent the infection

410
00:20:46,610 --> 00:20:52,230
by those biological agents.

411
00:20:52,230 --> 00:20:55,470
Section six focuses on laboratory hazards,

412
00:20:55,470 --> 00:20:57,030
in which case you have LAIs,

413
00:20:57,030 --> 00:20:58,830
laboratory acquired infections,

414
00:20:58,830 --> 00:21:00,870
the sources and the specimens,

415
00:21:00,870 --> 00:21:03,470
primary hazards and special hazards.

416
00:21:03,470 --> 00:21:05,670
In a laboratory setting,

417
00:21:05,670 --> 00:21:07,790
the culture volume of the biological agent

418
00:21:07,790 --> 00:21:11,430
is increased significantly due to various processes.

419
00:21:11,430 --> 00:21:15,630
You may also have instances where you have spills,

420
00:21:15,630 --> 00:21:17,350
accidents and incidents,

421
00:21:17,350 --> 00:21:19,070
and this increases the risk

422
00:21:19,070 --> 00:21:21,550
of laboratory acquired infections.

423
00:21:21,550 --> 00:21:24,230
The pathogen may also mutate in the laboratory

424
00:21:24,230 --> 00:21:26,670
and increase its infectivity.

425
00:21:26,670 --> 00:21:29,470
Now the sources and specimens is also another issue

426
00:21:29,470 --> 00:21:31,230
which must be considered.

427
00:21:31,230 --> 00:21:33,990
For instance, if you're working with patient samples,

428
00:21:33,990 --> 00:21:36,270
patient derived samples such as serum, blood,

429
00:21:36,270 --> 00:21:38,470
body fluids or tissue,

430
00:21:38,470 --> 00:21:41,870
there may be other laboratory hazards

431
00:21:41,870 --> 00:21:46,870
such as the presence of the other pathogens in the patient

432
00:21:46,890 --> 00:21:50,050
which have not been included in the report.

433
00:21:50,050 --> 00:21:55,320
And these can pose a risk to laboratory workers.

434
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:56,720
There are also special hazards

435
00:21:56,720 --> 00:21:58,760
which may be associated with the culture

436
00:21:58,760 --> 00:22:00,560
of that particular biological agent

437
00:22:00,560 --> 00:22:04,520
in a specific cycle or a specific working environment

438
00:22:04,520 --> 00:22:09,580
such as an enriched culture medium.

439
00:22:09,580 --> 00:22:12,220
Section seven focuses on exposure controls

440
00:22:12,220 --> 00:22:14,120
and personal protection.

441
00:22:14,120 --> 00:22:16,900
And this is basically where you address risk group,

442
00:22:16,900 --> 00:22:19,220
the classification of the biological agents

443
00:22:19,220 --> 00:22:20,820
into the four risk groups,

444
00:22:20,820 --> 00:22:24,060
the containment levels at your laboratory

445
00:22:24,060 --> 00:22:25,740
or your facility.

446
00:22:25,740 --> 00:22:29,140
So if you are working with risk group three and above,

447
00:22:29,140 --> 00:22:33,880
you need to have the appropriate BSL facility.

448
00:22:33,880 --> 00:22:36,020
And I will discuss this when we focus

449
00:22:36,020 --> 00:22:37,260
on engineering controls

450
00:22:37,260 --> 00:22:40,780
and the overall risk mitigation approach.

451
00:22:40,780 --> 00:22:44,020
Protective clothing, PPEs are very important

452
00:22:44,020 --> 00:22:45,400
and other protection.

453
00:22:46,320 --> 00:22:49,540
I've included this basic hierarchy over here

454
00:22:50,660 --> 00:22:53,620
to indicate the mitigation strategy

455
00:22:53,620 --> 00:22:56,500
which we will discuss in the lecture series

456
00:22:56,500 --> 00:23:00,750
on risk mitigation.

457
00:23:00,750 --> 00:23:03,750
Section eight focuses on handling and storage.

458
00:23:03,750 --> 00:23:05,570
So how do we handle spills?

459
00:23:05,570 --> 00:23:08,030
What is the spill cleanup, SOP?

460
00:23:08,030 --> 00:23:09,710
Or if there's a very large spill

461
00:23:09,710 --> 00:23:11,470
which is highly unlikely as you are working

462
00:23:11,470 --> 00:23:15,870
only in test tube levels or 50 ml or 100 ml of media.

463
00:23:15,870 --> 00:23:17,510
But if you have very large spills

464
00:23:17,510 --> 00:23:20,990
such as in environments which use a bioreactor

465
00:23:20,990 --> 00:23:23,190
or fermenter of 500 liters,

466
00:23:23,190 --> 00:23:26,750
is there a SOP for handling this contingency

467
00:23:26,750 --> 00:23:28,510
in case of a spill?

468
00:23:28,510 --> 00:23:29,990
Okay, that's what we look at.

469
00:23:29,990 --> 00:23:34,310
Disposal, what is the SOP for the disposal

470
00:23:34,310 --> 00:23:36,030
of that particular biological agent?

471
00:23:36,030 --> 00:23:38,230
Again, you have to look at the susceptibility

472
00:23:38,230 --> 00:23:41,590
of the biological agent to heat and chemical treatment.

473
00:23:41,590 --> 00:23:45,910
The general route is via the usage of incinerator

474
00:23:45,910 --> 00:23:50,570
or autoclaving system and effluent disposal and storage.

475
00:23:51,410 --> 00:23:54,730
Is the biological agent viable in storage?

476
00:23:54,730 --> 00:23:57,090
Or do you want to keep it viable in storage

477
00:23:57,090 --> 00:23:58,370
and maintain its viability

478
00:23:58,370 --> 00:24:00,570
for future downstream applications

479
00:24:00,570 --> 00:24:02,650
such as a vaccine development?

480
00:24:02,650 --> 00:24:04,770
Or do you want to destroy it

481
00:24:04,770 --> 00:24:09,770
and maintain it in the storage as a DNA or RNA,

482
00:24:10,170 --> 00:24:12,410
which is suitable for analysis?

483
00:24:12,410 --> 00:24:21,420
Section nine focuses on regulatory and other information.

484
00:24:21,940 --> 00:24:26,940
This information must be included in your PhDs.

485
00:24:26,940 --> 00:24:29,500
Now, every PhDs is a controlled document

486
00:24:29,500 --> 00:24:31,540
which is developed by the virus manager

487
00:24:31,540 --> 00:24:33,500
for that respective facility.

488
00:24:33,500 --> 00:24:35,220
For instance, if a biological agent

489
00:24:35,220 --> 00:24:38,580
has certain characteristics in a tropical country,

490
00:24:38,580 --> 00:24:41,580
the characteristics may be different in a temperate country

491
00:24:41,580 --> 00:24:44,380
and this must be taken into consideration.

492
00:24:44,380 --> 00:24:46,740
So the regulatory information must be included

493
00:24:46,740 --> 00:24:49,700
in the Ethrogen Safety Data Sheet,

494
00:24:49,700 --> 00:24:53,560
the date on which the information has been updated,

495
00:24:53,560 --> 00:24:56,260
the person who updated the information,

496
00:24:56,260 --> 00:24:57,940
and the current references.

497
00:24:57,940 --> 00:25:02,940
And this completes the PhDs template.

498
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:06,300
Now, in order to identify or determine

499
00:25:06,300 --> 00:25:10,860
if your biological agent belongs to a specific risk group

500
00:25:10,860 --> 00:25:13,460
or has been categorized into a specific risk group,

501
00:25:13,460 --> 00:25:16,620
all you need to do is visit the PhDs,

502
00:25:16,620 --> 00:25:20,460
key in the correct name of your biological agent,

503
00:25:20,460 --> 00:25:22,480
for example, Bacillus anthracis,

504
00:25:22,480 --> 00:25:26,780
and then you search for all of these information

505
00:25:26,780 --> 00:25:32,940
in the PhDs itself.

506
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:35,380
This brings us to the end of this particular lecture.

507
00:25:35,380 --> 00:25:36,420
So in this lecture,

508
00:25:36,420 --> 00:25:39,380
we have focused on microbiological risk assessment

509
00:25:39,380 --> 00:25:42,180
using the WHO-LBM as a reference.

510
00:25:42,180 --> 00:25:44,740
We have understood the scope of risk assessment

511
00:25:44,740 --> 00:25:47,420
as I indicated in the iceberg example.

512
00:25:47,420 --> 00:25:49,500
There are certain risks which are seen

513
00:25:49,500 --> 00:25:51,860
and certain risks which are unseen.

514
00:25:51,860 --> 00:25:56,860
And no matter how exhaustive the virus management strategy

515
00:25:57,900 --> 00:26:00,100
may be, there'll be always be certain risks

516
00:26:00,100 --> 00:26:02,540
which you cannot predict.

517
00:26:02,540 --> 00:26:04,380
However, your role as a virus manager

518
00:26:04,380 --> 00:26:08,180
is to ensure that the risk assessment is thorough

519
00:26:08,180 --> 00:26:09,880
and comprehensive.

520
00:26:09,880 --> 00:26:12,820
We have also looked at the Pathogen Safety Data Sheet.

521
00:26:12,820 --> 00:26:14,700
And again, I thank the government of Canada

522
00:26:14,700 --> 00:26:16,180
for this very useful reference,

523
00:26:16,180 --> 00:26:18,540
which has helped me over the past years

524
00:26:18,540 --> 00:26:22,700
and also helped thousands or hundreds of thousands

525
00:26:22,700 --> 00:26:25,420
of virus managers across the world.

526
00:26:25,420 --> 00:26:29,060
And we also looked at developing your own instrument.

527
00:26:29,060 --> 00:26:29,940
So at this stage,

528
00:26:29,940 --> 00:26:33,660
you should be able to develop your own instrument

529
00:26:33,660 --> 00:26:34,560
for risk assessment.

530
00:26:34,560 --> 00:26:36,780
This is in the form of a questionnaire.

531
00:26:36,780 --> 00:26:40,200
As a initiative on your behalf,

532
00:26:40,200 --> 00:26:43,260
I want you to develop your own instrument

533
00:26:43,260 --> 00:26:46,100
for risk assessment in your specific situation.

534
00:26:46,100 --> 00:26:48,000
Focus on the basic questions

535
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:51,580
which I have addressed in this particular lecture

536
00:26:51,580 --> 00:26:53,000
and deepen them.

537
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,800
For instance, if it says the biological agent

538
00:26:55,800 --> 00:27:00,080
is infected at a dosage of 15 CFU,

539
00:27:00,080 --> 00:27:01,920
you try and look deeper into that.

540
00:27:01,920 --> 00:27:04,520
Is it infected at that particular temperature

541
00:27:04,520 --> 00:27:06,160
and so on and so forth.

542
00:27:06,160 --> 00:27:10,280
So this is basically what I wanted to introduce to you.

543
00:27:10,280 --> 00:27:11,240
And in the next lecture,

544
00:27:11,240 --> 00:27:13,680
I will move on to risk mitigation.

545
00:27:13,680 --> 00:27:15,000
In risk mitigation,

546
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:17,880
I will discuss the application of the five controls

547
00:27:18,600 --> 00:27:21,400
to mitigate the risks posed by biological agents.

